WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
hello  my name is xenia gassic i'm a peach  breeder and geneticist at clemson  university  i would like to thank the organizers for  giving me the opportunity to present  breeding data management in specialty  crops in this presentation i will  introduce you to breeding information  management system or bims demonstrate  its features and discuss some future  works so what is bims bims is an online  tool which allows users to explore the  publicly available reading data in the  host community databases while also  providing a data management solution for  private breeding programs currently bims  is available freely in the four  specialty club databases database for  rosate paxinium citrus and pulse  and also in the cotton database this  slide features all of these databases  the four databases that serve 26  specialty crops had a farm gate value in  2019 of 19.4 billion  they represent 52 percent of all u.s  specialty crops pims is supported by  nrsp 10 project and newly submitted  proposal to scri and in addition to the  database home pages the databases can be  accessed from  nrsp.org and  breedvidpims.org this slide lists  current functionalities of bims some of  which will be demonstrated later in this  presentation  detailed description of bims and all its  functionalities will be provided in the  manuscript that is currently in revision  in the database journal  this paper describes how breeders can  use bims to manage their private  breeding data and provides detailed  examples among the current  functionalities are to create the  programs add members and control  permission levels  manage upload and edit genotype and  phenotype data to create your own data  fields to store and search data manage  the field book app  perform statistical analysis perform  searches with various categories and  trade cutoff values  and archive the entire data at any time  so why bins in addition to described  functionalities there are more  advantages to use bims  since bims reside in the community  databases curated public breeding data  can be imported to your private program  the data includes phenotype and genotype  data  curated genomic data can also be used in  your private program for example you can  search for genotype data using genomic  positions of markers without importing  those data into your private program for  collaborative projects all or part of  data can be easily released to public  and projects and another advantage of  bims is that bims is built in triple  triple is a toolkit for database  construction used by over 100 databases  worldwide  in triple in addition to core modules  users worldwide can develop and share  extension modules  currently over 40 extension modules are  available bims is developed as an  extension module which means any triple  database can use bims future analysis  tools developed by users worldwide in  addition to work we are doing in an rsv  10 project can be implemented in  community databases to be used with bims  there are three main areas in the bims  interface  the header region displays the selected  cropped and program  and has links to the configuration page  account page and the home page of the  community database the second part of  the interface is the accordion menu on  the left side  this menu allows users to switch between  various sections of bills  the last region is the tab region and  this is where users primarily interact  with bims each tab in bims has an  instruction section that provides  information on how to use that tab  without logging in users can choose a  crop and a public beams program to view  data and explore bims the about beams  and help menus provide various  information including links to the bim's  manual and mailing list to import data  into bims the data needs to be in the  bims templates that are available for  download in the template list under the  data import section of the left hand  accordion menu phenotype data can also  be imported to bims using exported files  from fieldbook the data templates tab  lists all the templates with hyperlinks  to view and download the template  users can also download all the  templates as a single excel file once  the data has been entered into the  templates  users can load the data using the upload  data link  in the upload data tab the file name  will appear in the uploaded files  section after a file has been submitted  to view the progress of an upload job  users can click on the file name and  click the view button the manage reading  section lets users view create or edit  data for trait location cross accession  and trial in addition to program  when users click for example trait  the manage trade tab opens and shows all  the trade descriptors in the trade  section  when there is imported public phenotype  data the descriptor groups of both  public data and private data are  displayed  the admin menu allows changing the order  of the trades adding manually new trade  or merging the same descriptor  in different descriptor groups clicking  one trait opens straight details section  on the right that shows details of the  trade descriptor along with the  distribution of the trade values and  statistical summary of the full data set  loaded to bims at the bottom of the  trade details section delete and edit  buttons are available  the search section allows users to  search phenotype genotype and cross data  clicking on phenotype the search by  phenotype tab appears this tab has three  sections choose property set filter and  phenotype search results the first step  to start a search is to select a  property or trait under the choose  property section for example users can  choose trial under properties  after the property is selected the set  filters section will populate with  options for that data type after  selecting the options user can click the  add button the selected filter will then  appear in the phenotype search result  section users can further filter the  search results by adding another  property or trait under the choose  properties section users can further  refine the search by adding thresholds  for a trait the set filter section will  show the data that are relevant for the  previous chosen filter for example the  statistics shown for the fruit l meaning  fruit length trait is for the accessions  from the trials chosen in the previous  search users can set the minimum and  maximum value of this trait and then  click add which then adds the new filter  to the phenotype search result section  any selected filters can be removed with  the remove button under each filter  under the results section the  descriptions menu can be expanded to  show the filters that were used to  generate the initial table the data  analysis section of the accordion menu  lets users further analyze phenotype  data  here multiple data sets could be chosen  using categories and compare the trait  values among the multiple data sets  when users click statistical analysis  under data analysis the statistical  analysis tab appears  the tab has three sections categories  traits and statistics  users can select a category under  category section  in this example data year and then  choose multiple datasets in the second  box that pops up once a category is  selected categories include the  accession list which is the list of  accessions that users saved in the  search section  after choosing multiple data sets users  can click the compare button  when the computation is done users can  select a trait in the middle section in  this example  ssc or sugar content the statistics  section on the right will show a graph  with the distribution of the trait  values for each data set  a table below the graph will show the  statistical values for each data set  users can click the legend to deselect  some of the data set fields to view  subsets  the fieldbook management section of the  accordion menu provides functionality to  generate input files for fieldbook or  load data to bins using the fieldbook  output files two types of files the  field file and trade file can be  generated using data in bins to load to  the fieldbook app the configure  fieldbook section allows users to  pre-select columns that can be included  in the field file  by default the three columns required in  the field file unique identifier primary  order and secondary order as well as  accessions are downloaded in the field  file  the fieldbook configuration page allows  other columns to be included in the  fieldbook input file so that fieldbook  users can see those in the main screen  of the fieldbook app the trade link  under the generate input file section  opens a page where it lists all the  trades stored in the program  users can select all or part of the  trades to generate the trade file for  the fieldbook app the field link under  the generate input files section opens a  page with the three choices  new trial existing trial and cross the  new trial section lets users choose one  of the accession lists that are  generated and saved in the search  section  choose additional columns and generate  the field file existing trial section  can be used when users want to generate  a newly updated field file for the  existing trial  the cross section allows breeders to  automatically generate progeny names and  generate an input file for a new cross  readers can upload a file with the cross  name row and the number of progeny for  each row for bims to generate a  fieldbook input file with new progeny  names  customization is one of the very  important and unique features of bits in  addition to being able to add new traits  at any time bims offers various ways to  customize the data management the  customization can be done following the  links in various pages under the  accordion menu  users can access them through the  configuration page link on the top right  of each bim's page  the bim's fieldbook link in the  configuration page opens a tab where  users can change the column names  required for the fieldbook app input  file  users can also add custom columns in  templates to store their  various metadata unique to their  breeding program  those custom columns can be selected to  be used as search categories as well  through the search phenotype cross  genotype category links  the fieldbook link on the configuration  page lets users choose which traits  accession properties or sample  properties will be displayed in the  generate field book input file section  and resulting field book input file  finally the trait order link in the  configuration page lets users change the  order of trades displayed in the  manage breeding page  data and beams programs are backed up  nightly  on-site and off-site for security  the archives section of the accordion  menu however provides functionality for  readers to archive data at any time all  archived files can be accessed in the  user's account page  one of the newest functionalities in  bims  is that it is brapi compliant  which means you can import data in  fieldbook app to bims just by clicking  buttons some of the future developments  are provided here including management  of image data which is almost done  next one that we are working on is to  make brims accessible to breeders  without community databases to  breedwithbims.org  through the nrsp10 project that is  funded we are working on supporting  phenomx data  genome-wide association analysis  capability  global performance prediction capability  to allow allow performance prediction  based on genotype and environment  also to develop further analysis tools  and breeding decision tools and to  identify sustainability models  in the submitted scr project which is  spending we are proposing to further  develop fieldbook app  do more curation of public phenotype and  genotype data for the four community  databases and on-site training for bim's  field book app use  this is all i have we want to thank our  sponsors for providing funds and  supporting development of this important  breeding database  and i hope these presentations had  piqued your interest in beams and you  will check it out  i will be happy to answer any questions  thank you for your attention

